hi everyone  my name is philip i'm a machine learning  engineer and hugging phase and the tech  lead for the aws  partnership welcome to this walkthrough  of the newest amazon search maker  features  in this walkthrough we are going to take  a look at the new  aws hanging face deep learning  containers  and the new hacking phase framework  extension for the python amazon  sagemaker sdk  we are going to fine-tune a text  classification transformer using the new  hyenface dlc and  sagemaker sdk the model will be trained  on the imdb  dataset for movie reviews before we get  started  um i wanted to let you know that you can  find the sample notebooks  and other sample notebooks and  documentation on hangingface.co  amazon there's also a detailed blog post  about the partnership with all the  benefits we are going to provide you  as customer  for our example we are using the amazon  search maker studio which is a web-based  machine learning ide  for training transformer model directly  in aws  with the integrated permission  management  so as you can see here right now i have  my notebook up and running  i'm running on a small t3 instance and  yeah let's get started you can find this  notebook as mentioned on the hugging  face  dot co slash amazon site and for our  example as mentioned we are  training an end-to-end binary text  classification model  using transformers and data sets for  training and pre-processing our data and  i will show you our fine tuning script  later  the first step we need to do is to to  install  the transformers library and the data  sets with the s3 extension  in our stagemaker studio  okay as we have now installed everything  we can  move forward the first and important  step is to to yeah to configure our  sagemaker session and get the right  credentials  in sagemaker you can define a sagemaker  session bucket  which if it is not created um sagemaker  will create it  and the sagemaker or this bucket is used  for  storing all your informations during  training  our data set will be uploaded to there  our trained model at the end will upload  it  there we have our logs and metrics will  also be located so if you  have a central point of all the  information for a training job  okay the next step is to to check if we  have um  yeah if our sagemaker hugging phase  integration is available  it is nice okay we have set up our  sagemaker session we created our session  backend we configured our um yeah  aws region where our training job will  run the next step is  pre-processing and our data in the new  extension you can either pre-process  your data in advance and provide  the pre-processed data set in your  training job  or you can do it inside a training job  in this example  i decided that we do the pre-processing  in advance  so here's a yeah a few code snippets  where we are going to load  our imdb dataset with the datasets  library  and load the tokenizer from the hub from  the hanging phase hub  create a helper function of humpty  function for tokenizing it  tokenizing our train and test data set  in the example i sample down the test  data set so we don't have  to wait um when doing training  and at the end we are just um yeah  setting the format to torch  since we are using pytorch as our base  framework for this example  um i will not do this right now  because it takes a few minutes and this  should be a very yeah  easy and light walk through but it works  after we have pre-processed our data set  we are going to use the new file system  integration for data sets  where we can directly save our  pre-processed  data set to s3 the only thing we need to  do is to define  yeah ns3 uri where the training set will  be located therefore we use our default  bucket  with a s3 prefix we defined earlier for  for naming our data set so we'll have  our sagemaker session bucket  slash imdb slash strain and for  for tests we are doing the same  okay so  as i i mentioned earlier we  introduced a new sdk it's very similar  to the existing  such as for tensorflow or for pytorch  the the new sdk is built not only by aws  we from the hugging phase side  also help them to build a nice sdk to  provide you developer the best  experience on fine tuning transformer  models on amazon sagemaker  and if you have any feedback or yeah  important news for us please let us know  the estimator  is basically a class instance of where  you define everything  sage maker needs for your training job  we have an entry point where  our train yeah script is located we  define our sagemaker session we can give  our training job a name we define the  instance type  on which the training will be run we  define the instance count so here you  can basically  scale up from one to as many instances  you want to run  distributed draining for example and  then we define our hyperparameters  which will be passed in as name  arguments so  you can imagine these dictionaries will  be converted to  this named argument for the training set  for our fine tuning script we are using  a  pretty basic example using the trainer  from transformers  and um yeah basic configuration  basically  a training script in sagemaker is a  normal python script with  main method and you the first  thing you need to do in your find unique  script is to to yet to set up  an argument parser since the  hyperparameters will be passed in as  named arguments if you don't have any  hyperparameters feel free to leave it  out  in our examples we are going to pass in  the epochs  the batch size and the model names for  most of the arguments i  defined default arguments but for the  model name for examples  i didn't define any defaults so we are  need to to yet to pass in the  hyperparameter for our model  into our fine tuning script  after we have passed our arguments um we  are setting up  yeah a logger to get all the  informations we want from our training  script sagemaker is  um forwarding the logs to our  cli and it's saving them to cloud watch  so  if you run your sagemaker job  asynchronously  which mean that you start and don't wait  until it's done  you can up look up your logs later in  cloudwatch  okay next step is load our data set as  um or since we have uploaded our data  sets to s3  sagemaker have downloaded them in  advance  on the machine where the training job is  run  and then we can use the load from disk  method from the data sets library to  load our pre-processed um  data sets that's it we are logging  information about the length then we  define a compute  function for for evaluation later  the next step is creating our model  instance  where we are using our passed in model  name parameter for  loading the model from the app then we  define our training arguments  which will be used in the training  here you can see we are also using the  epochs from  that we pass in the batch size and then  we create our trainer instance  with our model our arguments our custom  compute  metrics function pass in our train and  data set  and then we run trainer.train after  training is done we run trainer.evaluate  with our evaluation data set save the  results  in a text file which will be also  uploaded to s3 later  and then save our models to our model  directory  and that's basically it  okay so here you can see we have the the  hyper parameters we are going to pass in  and as model name we are using the  distal bird model  batch size of 16 running one epoch i  think it's enough for our  small little example then we we create  our estimator give it a last check on  our hyperparameters and then we can run  estimator.fit and pass in our train and  test input parameters and here train  input path is our s3 uri  to our directory on s3  that's it our training is now finished  um  as you can see sagemaker has forwarded  all the locks from our  training to the console if we would have  invoked or run the training  asynchronously  we could see these locks in cloudwatch  and here you can see uh sagemaker's  logging into the training environment  and we have all the environment  variables uh  which are accessible in our training  shop then we have  the locks from our training script where  you can see our  train data set length our test data set  length  and the metrics from our  yeah evaluation as a  evaluation accuracy we have 92 percent  and also the training took around 935  seconds which is roughly about  15 minutes so it's pretty nice um worth  mentioning is that you  only need to adjust like two lines of  code to use or to run  sagemaker distributed training and we  have integrated  data distribution directly into the  trainer and model library is also  integrated into a sagemaker  trainer i hope you like the walkthrough  if you have any questions or feedback  feel free to  open a new thread on our hanging face  forum or open a github issue  you

